// --- Lesson 1: The Songhai Empire — Government, Society, and Trade ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Songhai Empire: An African Empire of the 15th and 16th Centuries\n\nThe Songhai Empire (c. 1340-1591) was one of the largest and most powerful empires in African history. At its height in the late 1400s and early 1500s, it controlled a vast stretch of West Africa along the Niger River, covering parts of modern-day Mali, Niger, Nigeria, Senegal, and Guinea.\n\n### Origins\n\n- The Songhai people originally lived along the **Niger River** near the city of **Gao**\n- For centuries, they were subjects of the **Mali Empire**\n- As Mali weakened in the 14th century, the Songhai began to assert their independence\n- Around **1340**, the Songhai established their own dynasty and began expanding\n\n### Key Concept: What is an Empire?\n\nAn **empire** is a large political unit that rules over many different peoples and territories from a central authority. The Songhai Empire, like other empires of this period, combined military conquest with trade, religion, and administration to maintain control over diverse populations.\n\nThe Songhai Empire challenges the colonial myth that Africa had no complex civilisations before European contact. It demonstrates that Africans built sophisticated states with advanced systems of government, trade, education, and culture.'),
  q(2, 'What does the existence of the Songhai Empire challenge?',
    ['The colonial myth that Africa had no complex civilisations before European contact', 'The idea that empires can exist in West Africa', 'The belief that the Niger River was important for trade', 'The claim that Mali was once a powerful empire'], 0,
    'The Songhai Empire, along with other great African states like Mali and Ghana, proves that Africa had sophisticated civilisations with advanced government, trade networks, and centres of learning long before European colonisation. This challenges racist narratives that Africans were "primitive."'),
  t(3, '## Sonni Ali and the Rise of the Songhai Empire (1464-1492)\n\n**Sonni Ali** (also written Sunni Ali) ruled the Songhai Empire from **1464 to 1492** and transformed it from a small kingdom into a vast empire.\n\n### Military Conquests\n\n| Conquest | Date | Significance |\n|----------|------|-------------|\n| **Timbuktu** | 1468 | Major trading and learning centre; gave Songhai control of trans-Saharan trade |\n| **Djenne (Jenne)** | 1473 | After a 7-year siege; important commercial city and centre of Islamic scholarship |\n| **Niger River region** | 1460s-1490s | Controlled both banks, securing agriculture and river trade |\n\n### Government and Administration\n\n- Sonni Ali created a **centralised government** based in the capital **Gao**\n- He divided the empire into **provinces**, each governed by an appointed governor\n- He maintained a **professional army** and a powerful **river navy** on the Niger\n- He built an effective system of **tax collection** to fund the empire\n- He was both a Muslim and a practitioner of traditional African religion, which caused tension with Muslim scholars\n\n### Why Was Sonni Ali Important?\n\nSonni Ali was a brilliant military strategist who built the foundations of the Songhai Empire through conquest and centralised governance. His capture of Timbuktu and Djenne gave the empire control over the vital trans-Saharan trade routes and centres of Islamic learning.'),
  q(4, 'Which two cities did Sonni Ali capture to establish the Songhai Empire as a major power?',
    ['Timbuktu and Djenne', 'Gao and Mecca', 'Cairo and Marrakesh', 'Timbuktu and Mogadishu'], 0,
    'Sonni Ali captured Timbuktu in 1468 and Djenne in 1473 (after a 7-year siege). These cities were vital because they were major centres of trade and Islamic scholarship, giving the Songhai Empire control over the trans-Saharan trade network.'),
  t(5, '## Askia Muhammad and the Golden Age (1493-1528)\n\nAfter Sonni Ali died in 1492, his son was overthrown by one of his generals, **Muhammad Ture**, who took the title **Askia** (meaning "usurper" but later used as a royal title). Askia Muhammad ruled from **1493 to 1528** and brought the Songhai Empire to its greatest heights.\n\n### Government and Society Under Askia Muhammad\n\n- He divided the empire into **five provinces**, each with a governor (fari or farma)\n- He created **specialised government departments**:\n  - Treasury and tax collection\n  - Army command\n  - Navy command (for the Niger River fleet)\n  - Agriculture and forest management\n  - Religious affairs\n- He appointed **judges (qadis)** to enforce **Sharia (Islamic law)** in the cities\n- He promoted **Islam** as the official religion but allowed traditional beliefs in rural areas\n- He made the **pilgrimage to Mecca (hajj)** in 1496-1497, travelling with a large entourage and distributing gold along the way, which enhanced Songhai\'s international reputation\n\n### Social Structure\n\n| Class | Role |\n|-------|------|\n| **Askia (Emperor)** | Supreme ruler; political, military, and religious authority |\n| **Nobility and officials** | Provincial governors, military commanders, judges |\n| **Merchants** | Wealthy trading class; operated along trans-Saharan routes |\n| **Free citizens** | Farmers, artisans, fishermen |\n| **Enslaved people** | Captured in war; used in agriculture, domestic service, and trade |'),
  fb(6, 'Askia ___ ruled the Songhai Empire from 1493 to 1528 and brought it to its golden age. He undertook the ___ (pilgrimage to Mecca) in 1496-1497.',
    ['Muhammad', 'hajj'],
    'Askia Muhammad was a devout Muslim who promoted Islam throughout the empire. His pilgrimage to Mecca (hajj) in 1496-1497 established diplomatic relationships with other Islamic states and enhanced Songhai\'s prestige in the wider Muslim world.'),
  t(7, '## Trade and Commerce in the Songhai Empire\n\nThe Songhai Empire was positioned at the crossroads of major trade routes and became one of the wealthiest states in the world.\n\n### Trans-Saharan Trade\n\nThe most important trade routes crossed the **Sahara Desert**, connecting West Africa with North Africa, the Middle East, and Europe.\n\n| Exported from Songhai | Imported into Songhai |\n|-----------------------|----------------------|\n| **Gold** (from Akan goldfields) | **Salt** (from Saharan mines like Taghaza) |\n| **Enslaved people** | **Horses** (for the cavalry) |\n| **Ivory** | **Cloth and textiles** (from North Africa and Europe) |\n| **Kola nuts** | **Books and manuscripts** |\n| **Hides and leather** | **Weapons and metalwork** |\n\n### Timbuktu as a Trading Hub\n\n- **Timbuktu** was the most important commercial centre, where merchants from across the Islamic world gathered\n- **Arab, Italian, and Jewish merchants** traded in the city\'s markets\n- The city had a complex banking and credit system\n- Camel caravans of up to **12,000 camels** crossed the Sahara, carrying goods between Songhai and North Africa\n\n### The Role of Gold\n\nGold was the foundation of Songhai\'s wealth. West African gold supplied up to **two-thirds of the gold** circulating in the Mediterranean and European economies. This made Songhai an essential part of the global economy.'),
  q(8, 'What was the most important trade exchange in the trans-Saharan trade network?',
    ['Gold from West Africa exchanged for salt from the Saharan mines', 'Ivory from East Africa exchanged for spices from India', 'Cloth from Europe exchanged for copper from North Africa', 'Diamonds from southern Africa exchanged for weapons'], 0,
    'The gold-salt trade was the backbone of trans-Saharan commerce. West Africa had abundant gold but lacked salt (essential for food preservation in a hot climate), while the Sahara had vast salt deposits. Gold and salt were sometimes traded pound for pound.'),
  t(9, '## Learning and Culture in the Songhai Empire\n\nThe Songhai Empire, particularly the city of Timbuktu, was one of the great centres of learning in the medieval world.\n\n### The University of Sankore\n\n- The **University of Sankore** in Timbuktu was one of the most important centres of learning in the Islamic world\n- At its height, it had an estimated **25,000 students** — more than many European universities of the same period\n- Subjects taught included: **theology, law, astronomy, mathematics, medicine, history, and geography**\n- Scholars came from across the Islamic world to study and teach\n- The university produced some of the most important scholars in African history\n\n### Books and Manuscripts\n\n- Timbuktu was famous for its **book trade** — books were considered the most valuable commodity\n- **Leo Africanus**, an Arab traveller who visited in 1510, wrote: *"More profit is made from the sale of books than from any other merchandise"*\n- Thousands of manuscripts were produced and collected in Timbuktu, covering science, medicine, astronomy, law, and poetry\n- Many of these **Timbuktu Manuscripts** survive today and are preserved in libraries in Mali, proving the depth of African intellectual achievement\n\n### Key Scholars\n\n- **Ahmad Baba** (1556-1627): One of the most prolific scholars, who wrote over 40 books on law, theology, and biography\n- **Mahmud Kati**: Author of the **Tarikh al-Fattash**, a chronicle of the Songhai Empire'),
  fb(10, 'The University of ___ in Timbuktu was one of the most important centres of Islamic learning, with an estimated 25,000 students. According to traveller Leo Africanus, more profit was made from the sale of ___ than from any other merchandise in Timbuktu.',
    ['Sankore', 'books'],
    'The University of Sankore demonstrates that Timbuktu was a world-class centre of scholarship. The book trade shows that Songhai society highly valued learning and literacy, challenging colonial stereotypes about African civilisation.'),
  t(11, '## The Fall of the Songhai Empire: The Moroccan Invasion of 1591\n\nDespite its greatness, the Songhai Empire collapsed rapidly in the late 16th century.\n\n### Causes of Decline\n\n1. **Succession disputes**: After Askia Muhammad was deposed by his son in 1528, the empire suffered from power struggles and weak rulers\n2. **Civil wars**: Rival claimants to the throne fought each other, weakening central authority\n3. **Overextension**: The empire was too vast to control effectively with the technology of the time\n\n### The Moroccan Invasion\n\n- In **1591**, the Sultan of Morocco, **Ahmad al-Mansur**, sent an army across the Sahara to seize Songhai\'s gold resources\n- The Moroccan army was led by **Judar Pasha** and consisted of about **4,000 soldiers** armed with **muskets and cannons** — firearms that the Songhai did not possess\n- At the **Battle of Tondibi** (March 1591), the Songhai army (much larger but armed with swords, spears, and bows) was defeated by Moroccan firepower\n- Timbuktu, Gao, and Djenne were captured\n- Scholars were arrested and deported, many books were destroyed, and the centres of learning were devastated\n\n### Consequences\n\n- The Songhai Empire collapsed, and West Africa fragmented into smaller states\n- Trans-Saharan trade declined as trade routes shifted\n- The destruction of Timbuktu\'s scholarly community was a devastating loss for African intellectual heritage\n- The invasion shows the importance of **military technology** in determining the outcomes of encounters between civilisations'),
  q(12, 'What was the key military advantage that allowed the Moroccans to defeat the much larger Songhai army at the Battle of Tondibi in 1591?',
    ['The Moroccans had muskets and cannons (firearms), which the Songhai did not possess', 'The Moroccans had a much larger army', 'The Songhai soldiers refused to fight', 'The Moroccans used elephants in battle'], 0,
    'The Battle of Tondibi (1591) illustrates how military technology can determine the outcome of conflict. The Songhai army was much larger but had no firearms. The Moroccans, armed with muskets and cannons acquired from Europe, won a decisive victory despite being outnumbered.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The Ming Dynasty (China) ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Ming Dynasty: A World Power in the 14th and 15th Centuries (1368-1644)\n\nWhile the Songhai Empire flourished in West Africa, the **Ming Dynasty** ruled China as one of the most powerful and advanced civilisations in the world. At its height, Ming China had a population of around **200 million people** — far more than all of Europe combined.\n\n### Origins of the Ming Dynasty\n\n- The Ming Dynasty was founded by **Zhu Yuanzhang**, a peasant rebel leader who overthrew the Mongol **Yuan Dynasty** in **1368**\n- He took the title **Hongwu Emperor** and established the capital at **Nanjing** (later moved to **Beijing** by his successor)\n- The word "Ming" means **"brilliant"** or "radiant"\n- The Hongwu Emperor restored Chinese rule after nearly a century of Mongol domination\n\n### Government and Administration\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Emperor** | Absolute ruler; "Son of Heaven" with supreme authority |\n| **Civil service** | Highly trained bureaucracy selected through rigorous examinations |\n| **Examination system** | Officials chosen by merit through competitive exams based on Confucian texts |\n| **Provinces** | Empire divided into 15 provinces, each with appointed governors |\n| **Censorate** | Officials who monitored government corruption |\n| **Military** | Large standing army; defensive focus (Great Wall reconstruction) |'),
  q(2, 'How were government officials selected in Ming China?',
    ['Through a competitive civil service examination system based on Confucian texts', 'By hereditary succession from father to son', 'By popular election among citizens', 'By appointment of the strongest military generals'], 0,
    'The Ming civil service examination system was remarkably meritocratic for its time. Officials were selected based on their knowledge of Confucian classics, philosophy, and literature. This system produced highly educated administrators, though in practice it favoured wealthy families who could afford years of education.'),
  t(3, '## Ming Society\n\n### Social Structure\n\nMing society was organised into a strict hierarchy based on **Confucian principles**:\n\n| Class | Role |\n|-------|------|\n| **Emperor and imperial family** | Supreme authority; divine mandate to rule |\n| **Scholar-officials (mandarins)** | Government administrators; highest social status |\n| **Farmers (peasants)** | The backbone of society; produced food; about 85% of the population |\n| **Artisans** | Skilled craftsmen — porcelain makers, silk weavers, metalworkers |\n| **Merchants** | Despite their wealth, ranked lowest in the Confucian social order |\n\n**Confucianism** taught that society functioned best when everyone fulfilled their proper role. The emperor ruled justly, officials served honestly, and subjects obeyed authority. This philosophy emphasised **harmony, duty, education, and respect for elders**.\n\n### Agriculture\n\n- Rice was the staple food; China produced more rice than any other country\n- **New crops** from the Americas (introduced later via trade) included sweet potatoes, maize, and peanuts\n- Advanced irrigation systems and farming techniques supported a massive population\n- Most Chinese people were peasant farmers who paid taxes in grain'),
  fb(4, 'In the Ming social hierarchy, ___-officials ranked highest in social status after the emperor. The philosophy of ___ emphasised harmony, duty, education, and respect for elders.',
    ['Scholar', 'Confucianism'],
    'Scholar-officials (mandarins) who passed the civil service examinations held the highest social status in Ming China. Confucianism was the dominant philosophy that shaped Chinese society, government, and culture for over two thousand years.'),
  t(5, '## Trade, Ship Building, and the Treasure Fleet Expeditions of Zheng He\n\nThe most remarkable aspect of Ming China\'s engagement with the wider world was the **Treasure Fleet** voyages of **Zheng He** from 1405 to 1433.\n\n### Who Was Zheng He?\n\n- Zheng He (1371-1433) was a **Muslim eunuch** of Mongol descent who served the **Yongle Emperor**\n- He was captured as a boy during the Ming conquest and raised in the imperial court\n- Despite his origins, he rose to become the greatest naval commander in Chinese history\n\n### The Treasure Fleet\n\n- Zheng He commanded a fleet of up to **300 ships** and **28,000 men**\n- His largest ships, the **treasure ships (bao chuan)**, were up to **120 metres long** — the largest wooden ships ever built\n- By comparison, Columbus\'s flagship the Santa Maria (1492) was only about **25 metres long**\n- The fleet carried **silk, porcelain, tea, gold, and silver** as trade goods and diplomatic gifts\n\n### The Seven Voyages (1405-1433)\n\n| Voyage | Dates | Destinations |\n|--------|-------|-------------|\n| 1st-3rd | 1405-1411 | Southeast Asia, India, Sri Lanka |\n| 4th | 1413-1415 | Persian Gulf, Hormuz |\n| 5th-6th | 1417-1422 | East Africa (Mogadishu, Malindi, Mombasa) |\n| 7th | 1431-1433 | Final voyage to Arabia and East Africa |\n\nZheng He\'s voyages reached **East Africa** decades before the Portuguese — proving that Chinese mariners had mapped and navigated the Indian Ocean trade routes long before European "exploration."'),
  q(6, 'How did the size of Zheng He\'s treasure ships compare to Columbus\'s Santa Maria?',
    ['Zheng He\'s ships were up to 120 metres long, compared to Columbus\'s 25-metre Santa Maria', 'They were roughly the same size', 'Columbus\'s ships were much larger', 'Zheng He used small fishing boats'], 0,
    'The comparison is striking: Zheng He\'s treasure ships were up to five times longer than Columbus\'s Santa Maria. This shows that Chinese naval technology in the early 15th century was far more advanced than European technology. The fleet carried 28,000 men across 300 ships.'),
  t(7, '## Scientific and Cultural Achievements of the Ming Dynasty\n\nMing China produced remarkable advances in science, technology, and culture.\n\n### Technology and Science\n\n| Achievement | Detail |\n|------------|--------|\n| **Compass** | The magnetic compass, invented in China, was essential for navigation; Zheng He\'s fleet relied on it |\n| **Gunpowder** | Originally a Chinese invention, used for weapons, fireworks, and mining |\n| **Printing** | Movable-type printing (invented earlier in China) enabled the mass production of books |\n| **Porcelain** | Ming blue-and-white porcelain was prized worldwide; the word "china" became synonymous with fine porcelain |\n| **Medicine** | Li Shizhen compiled the **Bencao Gangmu** (1578), a comprehensive pharmacopoeia listing over 1,800 medicines |\n| **Architecture** | The **Forbidden City** in Beijing was built during the Ming era (1406-1420) — the largest palace complex in the world |\n\n### The Great Wall\n\n- The Ming Dynasty rebuilt and extended the **Great Wall of China** to its present form\n- The wall stretched approximately **6,000 kilometres** along China\'s northern border\n- Its purpose was to defend against Mongol invasions from the north\n- It required enormous labour and resources — demonstrating the power of the centralised state\n\n### Culture\n\n- Ming China produced some of the greatest works of Chinese literature, including the novel **Journey to the West**\n- The arts flourished: painting, calligraphy, poetry, and opera\n- Silk production was a major industry; Chinese silk was traded across the world'),
  fb(8, 'Ming blue-and-white ___ was so prized that the word "china" became synonymous with fine ceramics. The ___ City in Beijing, built during the Ming era, is the largest palace complex in the world.',
    ['porcelain', 'Forbidden'],
    'Ming porcelain, especially the distinctive blue-and-white style, was exported across the world and became one of China\'s most iconic cultural achievements. The Forbidden City, home to 24 emperors, demonstrated the power and grandeur of Ming imperial authority.'),
  t(9, '## China Looks Inwards After 1433\n\nAfter 1433, China made a dramatic decision that changed the course of world history: it turned away from maritime exploration and trade.\n\n### Why Did China Turn Inward?\n\n1. **Cost**: The Treasure Fleet voyages were enormously expensive, and the new emperor\'s advisors argued the money was better spent on defence against the Mongols\n2. **Confucian ideology**: Scholar-officials looked down on trade and foreign contact; they believed China was self-sufficient and had nothing to learn from "barbarians"\n3. **Internal threats**: The Mongol threat on the northern frontier demanded military resources (invested in the Great Wall instead)\n4. **Political change**: After the Yongle Emperor died (1424), subsequent emperors were less interested in maritime exploration\n5. **Bureaucratic opposition**: Scholar-officials who opposed the voyages gained influence and had Zheng He\'s records destroyed\n\n### The Haijin (Sea Ban) Policy\n\n- The **Haijin** restricted private overseas trade and coastal activity\n- Ship-building for large vessels was banned\n- Chinese merchants who traded overseas could be executed\n- Coastal populations were sometimes forced to move inland\n\n### Consequences\n\n- China withdrew from the Indian Ocean just as European powers (Portugal, Spain) began their own maritime expansion\n- By the time Europeans arrived in Asian waters, there was no Chinese naval presence to challenge them\n- Some historians argue this was the moment when Europe began to overtake China as the dominant global power\n- This raises a key historical question: **What might have happened if China had continued its maritime expansion?**'),
  q(10, 'What was the significance of China\'s decision to turn inward after 1433?',
    ['It left the Indian Ocean open for European maritime powers to dominate global trade', 'It had no effect on world history', 'It led to China conquering more territory in Asia', 'It caused the immediate collapse of the Ming Dynasty'], 0,
    'China\'s withdrawal from the seas after 1433 was a pivotal moment in world history. When European powers began exploring the Indian Ocean in the late 1400s, they faced no Chinese naval competition. This allowed Portugal and later other European nations to dominate the sea routes and build colonial empires.'),
];

// --- Lesson 3: The Mughal Empire (India) ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson3 = [
  t(1, '## The Mughal Empire: Government and Society (1526-1858)\n\nThe **Mughal Empire** was one of the largest and most powerful empires in Indian history. At its height in the 17th century, it controlled most of the Indian subcontinent and had a population of over **150 million people** — making it one of the most populous states in the world.\n\n### Origins\n\n- The Mughal Empire was founded by **Babur** (1483-1530), a descendant of both **Genghis Khan** (Mongol) and **Timur** (Turk)\n- The word "Mughal" is derived from "Mongol"\n- In **1526**, Babur invaded northern India and defeated the Sultan of Delhi at the **Battle of Panipat**, using **cannons and firearms** — technology that Indian armies lacked\n- Babur established the Mughal Dynasty with its capital at **Agra**\n\n### Government Under the Great Mughals\n\n| Emperor | Reign | Key Achievements |\n|---------|-------|------------------|\n| **Babur** | 1526-1530 | Founded the empire; introduced firearms to India |\n| **Humayun** | 1530-1556 | Lost and regained the empire |\n| **Akbar** | 1556-1605 | Greatest Mughal; expanded empire; promoted religious tolerance |\n| **Jahangir** | 1605-1627 | Patron of arts; expansion continued |\n| **Shah Jahan** | 1627-1658 | Built the Taj Mahal; golden age of Mughal architecture |\n| **Aurangzeb** | 1658-1707 | Expanded empire to greatest extent but caused its decline through intolerance |'),
  q(2, 'Who founded the Mughal Empire and what was his key military advantage?',
    ['Babur, who used cannons and firearms that Indian armies lacked', 'Akbar, who had the largest cavalry in Asia', 'Shah Jahan, who built impregnable fortresses', 'Aurangzeb, who had a massive navy'], 0,
    'Babur defeated the much larger army of the Sultan of Delhi at the Battle of Panipat (1526) largely because he possessed cannons and firearms obtained from the Ottoman Turks. This is another example of how military technology shaped history, similar to the Moroccan invasion of Songhai.'),
  t(3, '## Akbar the Great: Government and Religious Tolerance\n\n**Akbar** (r. 1556-1605) is considered the greatest Mughal emperor because of his administrative reforms and religious tolerance.\n\n### Government System\n\n- Akbar created an efficient **centralised administration** called the **mansabdari system**\n- Every official was assigned a **mansab** (rank) that determined their salary and the number of soldiers they maintained\n- He divided the empire into **provinces (subahs)** governed by appointed officials\n- He reformed **tax collection**: taxes were assessed based on the average crop yield over 10 years, making the system fairer to farmers\n- He abolished the **jizya** — a tax imposed on non-Muslims — as a gesture of religious tolerance\n\n### Religious Policy: Din-i-Ilahi\n\n- Akbar was a Muslim who practised remarkable **religious tolerance** in a diverse empire\n- He married Hindu princesses and appointed Hindu generals and officials (e.g., **Raja Man Singh**)\n- He held discussions with scholars of all religions: Muslims, Hindus, Christians, Zoroastrians, and Jains\n- He created a new syncretic faith called **Din-i-Ilahi** ("Divine Faith"), blending elements of various religions\n- His policy of **sulh-i-kul** ("universal peace") promoted harmony between religious communities\n\nAkbar\'s religious tolerance was unusual for his time and stands in contrast to the religious conflicts occurring in Europe during the same period.'),
  fb(4, 'Akbar abolished the ___, a tax imposed on non-Muslims, as a gesture of religious tolerance. His policy of ___-i-kul ("universal peace") promoted harmony between different religious communities.',
    ['jizya', 'sulh'],
    'The jizya was a discriminatory tax that non-Muslims (primarily Hindus in India) had to pay. Akbar\'s abolition of this tax was revolutionary because it treated all subjects equally regardless of religion. His policy of sulh-i-kul ("universal peace") was remarkably ahead of its time.'),
  t(5, '## Trade in the Indian Ocean and Islamic World\n\nThe Mughal Empire was deeply integrated into the **Indian Ocean trade network**, one of the most important commercial systems in the pre-modern world.\n\n### Indian Ocean Trade\n\n| Exported from Mughal India | Imported into Mughal India |\n|---------------------------|---------------------------|\n| **Cotton textiles** (the most important export) | **Gold and silver** (from Europe and the Americas) |\n| **Spices** (pepper, cinnamon, cardamom) | **Horses** (from Central Asia and Arabia) |\n| **Indigo dye** | **Chinese porcelain and silk** |\n| **Diamonds and precious stones** | **European weapons and clocks** |\n| **Sugar** | **Coffee** (from Yemen) |\n\n### Key Trading Partners\n\n- **Arab merchants**: Centuries-old trade connections across the Indian Ocean\n- **Southeast Asia**: Trade in spices, tin, and textiles\n- **East Africa**: Trade in gold, ivory, and enslaved people\n- **European traders**: The **Portuguese** arrived in 1498 (Vasco da Gama); later joined by the **Dutch**, **English**, and **French**\n\n### The Importance of Textiles\n\nIndian cotton textiles were the **most traded commodity in the Indian Ocean** world. Indian weavers produced cloth of unmatched quality and variety. Indian textiles were traded as far as East Africa, Southeast Asia, and Europe. This massive textile industry made India one of the wealthiest regions in the world.'),
  q(6, 'What was the most important export from Mughal India?',
    ['Cotton textiles', 'Spices', 'Diamonds', 'Gold'], 0,
    'Indian cotton textiles were the most traded commodity in the Indian Ocean world. Indian weavers produced cloth of extraordinary quality and variety that was in demand across Africa, Asia, and Europe. This textile trade was a key source of Mughal India\'s wealth.'),
  t(7, '## Astronomy, Technology, and the Seamless Celestial Globe\n\nThe Mughal Empire made significant contributions to science and technology, blending Indian, Islamic, and Central Asian knowledge traditions.\n\n### Astronomy\n\n- Mughal rulers were patrons of **astronomical observatories**\n- Astronomers built large instruments to observe and map the stars\n- **Jai Singh II** (early 18th century) built five observatories called **Jantar Mantar** across India, including one in Delhi and one in Jaipur that still stand today\n- These observatories contained massive stone instruments used to measure time, predict eclipses, and track celestial bodies\n\n### The Seamless Celestial Globe\n\nOne of the most remarkable technological achievements of the Mughal period was the **seamless celestial globe**:\n\n- Made in **Kashmir in 1589** by the metalworker **Ali Kashmiri ibn Luqman**\n- A hollow metal globe depicting the stars and constellations, cast in a single piece without any seams or joints\n- This was thought to be **metallurgically impossible** until modern scientists studied the globe\n- It demonstrated advanced knowledge of **metallurgy, astronomy, and mathematics**\n- Only a handful of these globes survive, held in museums around the world\n\nThe seamless celestial globe is evidence of the sophisticated scientific and technological knowledge that existed in the Mughal Empire — knowledge that European scientists did not replicate for centuries.'),
  fb(8, 'The seamless celestial ___ was made in Kashmir in 1589 and was cast in a single piece without any joints. The Jantar ___ observatories were built to measure time and track celestial bodies.',
    ['globe', 'Mantar'],
    'The seamless celestial globe demonstrates remarkable metallurgical skill — scientists long believed it was impossible to cast a hollow metal globe without seams. The Jantar Mantar observatories contained sophisticated astronomical instruments that rivalled European technology of the same period.'),
  t(9, '## Architecture in the 16th and 17th Centuries: The Taj Mahal\n\nMughal architecture is considered among the finest in the world, blending **Islamic, Persian, and Indian** architectural traditions.\n\n### The Taj Mahal\n\n- Built by **Shah Jahan** between **1631 and 1653** in **Agra** as a mausoleum (tomb) for his beloved wife **Mumtaz Mahal**, who died during childbirth\n- It took over **20,000 workers** and **22 years** to complete\n- Made primarily of white **marble** inlaid with precious and semi-precious stones (pietra dura technique)\n- The building is perfectly **symmetrical** and features:\n  - A central dome 73 metres high\n  - Four minarets\n  - Elaborate gardens with reflecting pools\n  - Calligraphy from the Quran\n- It is considered one of the **greatest architectural masterpieces** in the world and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site\n\n### Other Mughal Architectural Achievements\n\n- **Red Fort** in Delhi: The imperial residence, built of red sandstone\n- **Fatehpur Sikri**: Akbar\'s abandoned capital city, a masterpiece of planned urban design\n- **Jama Masjid** in Delhi: One of the largest mosques in India\n\nMughal architecture demonstrates the wealth, power, and cultural sophistication of the empire. The blend of Hindu and Islamic artistic traditions reflects the cultural diversity of Mughal India.'),
  q(10, 'Why was the Taj Mahal built?',
    ['As a mausoleum (tomb) for Shah Jahan\'s wife Mumtaz Mahal, who died during childbirth', 'As a fortress to defend against invaders', 'As a palace for the Mughal emperor', 'As a university for Islamic scholarship'], 0,
    'The Taj Mahal was built as an expression of Shah Jahan\'s grief and love for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, who died in 1631 while giving birth to their 14th child. It took 22 years and over 20,000 workers to complete and is considered one of the most beautiful buildings ever constructed.'),
  t(11, '## Britain and the End of the Mughal Empire\n\nThe Mughal Empire declined gradually from the early 18th century and was eventually replaced by British colonial rule.\n\n### Causes of Decline\n\n1. **Aurangzeb\'s intolerance** (r. 1658-1707): Reversed Akbar\'s religious tolerance; reimposed the jizya tax; destroyed Hindu temples; provoked rebellions (especially the **Marathas**)\n2. **Succession wars**: After Aurangzeb\'s death, weak emperors fought each other for the throne\n3. **Regional breakaway**: Provinces became increasingly independent (Hyderabad, Bengal, Awadh)\n4. **Foreign invasions**: Nadir Shah of Persia sacked Delhi in **1739**, carrying away the Mughal treasury including the famous Peacock Throne\n\n### The British East India Company\n\n- The **British East India Company** (founded 1600) gradually expanded from trading posts to territorial control\n- The **Battle of Plassey (1757)**: Robert Clive defeated the Nawab of Bengal, giving the Company control of Bengal\'s wealth\n- The Company used **divide and rule** tactics, playing Indian rulers against each other\n- By the early 19th century, the Mughal emperor was little more than a puppet of the British\n\n### The End\n\n- The **Indian Rebellion of 1857** (also called the Sepoy Mutiny) was a major uprising against British rule\n- After crushing the rebellion, Britain **exiled the last Mughal emperor, Bahadur Shah Zafar**, to Burma\n- The British Crown took direct control of India, beginning the period known as the **British Raj** (1858-1947)\n\nThe fall of the Mughal Empire illustrates how European trading companies used military power and political manipulation to establish colonial control over wealthy Asian societies.'),
  q(12, 'How did the British East India Company gain control of India?',
    ['Through military victories (like Plassey), divide-and-rule tactics, and gradual territorial expansion from trading posts', 'Through a single decisive military invasion', 'Through a peaceful trade agreement with the Mughal emperor', 'By purchasing India from the Portuguese'], 0,
    'The British conquest of India was a gradual process. The East India Company started as traders but used military force, diplomacy, and divide-and-rule tactics to expand its control. The Battle of Plassey (1757) was a turning point, giving the Company control of Bengal\'s enormous wealth.'),
];

