// --- Lesson 1: Spanish Conquest of the Americas ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## European Expansion and Conquest: The 15th to 18th Centuries\n\nFrom the late 15th century, European powers began a process of **exploration, conquest, and colonisation** that would transform the world. This process brought Europeans into contact — and conflict — with the great civilisations we have already studied.\n\n### Why Did Europeans Expand?\n\n1. **Economic motives**: Europeans wanted direct access to the luxury goods of Asia (spices, silk, gold) without paying Arab and Ottoman middlemen\n2. **Religious motives**: The desire to spread Christianity ("saving souls") and defeat Islam\n3. **Political competition**: European nations competed with each other for power, territory, and prestige\n4. **Technology**: Advances in ship design (the **caravel**), navigation (compass, astrolabe), and weapons (cannons, firearms) made long-distance voyaging possible\n5. **Renaissance curiosity**: The spirit of inquiry encouraged exploration of the unknown\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| **Colonialism** | The practice of one country establishing control over another territory, exploiting its people and resources |\n| **Conquest** | Taking control of territory by military force |\n| **Imperialism** | A broader term for one country extending power over others through various means |\n| **Indigenous peoples** | The original inhabitants of a territory before colonisation |'),
  q(2, 'What was the main economic motive for European expansion in the 15th century?',
    ['To gain direct access to Asian luxury goods without paying Arab and Ottoman middlemen', 'To find new farming land for growing populations', 'To escape religious persecution in Europe', 'To establish democratic governments in new territories'], 0,
    'European merchants were frustrated by the high prices charged by Arab and Ottoman middlemen who controlled the overland trade routes to Asia. Finding a sea route to Asia would allow Europeans to buy spices, silk, and other luxury goods directly, at much greater profit.'),
  t(3, '## Case Study: The Spanish Conquest of the Americas\n\n### Christopher Columbus and the "Discovery" of the Americas\n\n- In **1492**, the Spanish monarchs Ferdinand and Isabella funded **Christopher Columbus** to find a western sea route to Asia\n- Instead, Columbus landed in the **Caribbean** (the Bahamas), which he mistakenly believed was part of Asia\n- He called the indigenous people "Indians" — a name that stuck despite being incorrect\n- Columbus made **four voyages** (1492-1504) but never realised he had reached a continent unknown to Europeans\n- His arrival began a process of **conquest, colonisation, and destruction** of indigenous American civilisations\n\n### Important Note\n\nColumbus did not "discover" the Americas — indigenous peoples had lived there for at least **15,000 years** and had built complex civilisations. The term "discovery" reflects a **Eurocentric** perspective that ignores the existence and achievements of these peoples.\n\n### The Aztec Empire\n\nWhen the Spanish arrived, the **Aztec Empire** (also called the Mexica) ruled much of central Mexico from its capital **Tenochtitlan** (modern Mexico City):\n- Population of Tenochtitlan: approximately **200,000-300,000** — larger than any European city at the time\n- Built on an island in Lake Texcoco with causeways, canals, aqueducts, and temples\n- Advanced agricultural system using **chinampas** (floating gardens)\n- Complex religious system that included human sacrifice'),
  fb(4, 'In 1492, ___ sailed west from Spain hoping to reach Asia but instead landed in the Caribbean. He mistakenly called the indigenous peoples "___."',
    ['Columbus', 'Indians'],
    'Columbus never realised he had reached a continent unknown to Europeans. His misnaming of indigenous peoples as "Indians" reflects Eurocentric ignorance of the existing civilisations of the Americas, which had flourished for thousands of years before European contact.'),
  t(5, '## Hernan Cortes and the Conquest of the Aztec Empire (1519-1521)\n\n### The Conquest\n\n- In **1519**, the Spanish conquistador **Hernan Cortes** landed on the coast of Mexico with approximately **500 soldiers**, 16 horses, and a few cannons\n- Despite being vastly outnumbered, Cortes defeated the Aztec Empire within two years\n\n### How Did the Spanish Win?\n\n1. **Superior weapons**: Steel swords, armour, crossbows, cannons, and muskets vs obsidian blades and wooden shields\n2. **Horses**: The Aztecs had never seen horses; mounted soldiers terrified them\n3. **Indigenous allies**: Cortes allied with peoples who resented Aztec rule (especially the **Tlaxcalans**), gaining tens of thousands of additional fighters\n4. **Disease**: The Spanish unwittingly brought **smallpox**, measles, and other diseases to which indigenous peoples had no immunity. Smallpox killed up to **50% of the Aztec population** during the siege of Tenochtitlan\n5. **Political manipulation**: Cortes exploited divisions among indigenous groups and took the Aztec emperor **Montezuma II** hostage\n\n### The Fall of Tenochtitlan (1521)\n\n- Cortes besieged Tenochtitlan for **75 days**\n- The city was devastated by fighting, starvation, and smallpox\n- An estimated **240,000 Aztecs died** during the siege\n- The Spanish demolished Tenochtitlan and built **Mexico City** on its ruins\n- The last Aztec emperor, **Cuauhtemoc**, was captured and later executed'),
  q(6, 'What was the most devastating factor in the Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire?',
    ['European diseases like smallpox, which killed up to 50% of the Aztec population', 'The superior numbers of the Spanish army', 'The weakness of the Aztec military', 'A volcanic eruption that destroyed Tenochtitlan'], 0,
    'Disease was the deadliest weapon of European conquest. Smallpox and other diseases were completely new to the Americas, and indigenous peoples had no immunity. Some historians estimate that European diseases killed up to 90% of the indigenous population of the Americas within a century of contact.'),
  t(7, '## The Impact of Colonisation and Slavery in the Americas\n\n### How Colonisation Led to Slavery\n\nThe Spanish conquest created a demand for forced labour:\n\n1. **Encomienda system**: Indigenous peoples were forced to work for Spanish landowners in return for "protection" and Christian instruction — in reality, it was a form of slavery\n2. **Mining**: The Spanish discovered vast silver deposits at **Potosi** (Bolivia) and forced indigenous labourers to work in deadly conditions\n3. **Population collapse**: European diseases killed so many indigenous people that the Spanish turned to **Africa** for enslaved labour\n4. **The Transatlantic Slave Trade began**: From the early 1500s, millions of Africans were kidnapped, transported across the Atlantic, and forced to work on **plantations** (sugar, tobacco, cotton) and in mines\n\n### The Impact on Indigenous Societies\n\n| Impact | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Population collapse** | The indigenous population of the Americas fell by an estimated 90% in the century after 1492 (from c. 60 million to 6 million) |\n| **Cultural destruction** | Spanish missionaries destroyed indigenous religious texts, temples, and artefacts |\n| **Land seizure** | Indigenous peoples lost their lands to Spanish settlers |\n| **Forced conversion** | Indigenous peoples were forced to convert to Christianity |\n| **Language loss** | Spanish replaced many indigenous languages |\n\n### The Impact on the World\n\n- The wealth extracted from the Americas (especially silver from Potosi) financed Spanish and European economic growth\n- The **Columbian Exchange**: Plants, animals, and diseases moved between the Americas, Europe, Africa, and Asia, transforming diets and economies worldwide\n- The Transatlantic Slave Trade became one of the greatest crimes in human history'),
  fb(8, 'The ___ system forced indigenous peoples in the Americas to work for Spanish landowners. The indigenous population of the Americas fell by an estimated ___% in the century after 1492.',
    ['encomienda', '90'],
    'The encomienda system was a form of forced labour that was slavery in all but name. The catastrophic population decline (estimated 90%) was caused primarily by European diseases like smallpox, measles, and influenza, to which indigenous peoples had no immunity.'),
  q(9, 'What was the "Columbian Exchange"?',
    ['The transfer of plants, animals, diseases, and ideas between the Americas, Europe, Africa, and Asia after 1492', 'A trade agreement between Spain and Portugal', 'A system of currency exchange between European merchants', 'The name for Columbus\'s four voyages to the Americas'], 0,
    'The Columbian Exchange was the widespread transfer of plants (potatoes, maize, tomatoes, tobacco to Europe; wheat, sugar, horses to the Americas), animals, diseases, and ideas between the Old World and the New World. It transformed diets, economies, and populations on both sides of the Atlantic.'),
  t(10, '## South African Connection: The Dutch East India Company and the Cape\n\nThe European expansion that devastated the Americas also reached southern Africa.\n\n### The Dutch East India Company (VOC)\n\n- The **Vereenigde Oost-Indische Compagnie (VOC)** was founded in the Netherlands in **1602**\n- It was the world\'s first **multinational corporation** and the first to issue shares on a stock exchange\n- The VOC traded in spices, silk, and other Asian goods, and needed a **halfway refreshment station** between Europe and Asia\n\n### The Cape Colony\n\n- In **1652**, **Jan van Riebeeck** arrived at the **Cape of Good Hope** to establish a refreshment station for VOC ships\n- Initially, the VOC did not intend to colonise — they just wanted fresh water, meat, and vegetables for their ships\n- However, the settlement expanded as Dutch **free burghers** (free citizens released from VOC service) began farming\n- Conflict with the **Khoikhoi** (indigenous pastoralists) began almost immediately as settlers seized grazing land\n- The VOC imported **enslaved people** from Madagascar, Mozambique, Indonesia, and India to work on farms and in the settlement\n\n### Consequences\n\nThe establishment of the Cape Colony began a process of land dispossession, forced labour, and racial domination that would shape South African history for the next 350 years. The patterns of colonialism visible in the Americas — land seizure, forced labour, cultural destruction — were repeated at the Cape.'),
  q(11, 'Why did the Dutch East India Company (VOC) establish a settlement at the Cape in 1652?',
    ['To create a halfway refreshment station for VOC ships travelling between Europe and Asia', 'To conquer the indigenous peoples of southern Africa', 'To find gold and diamonds', 'To escape religious persecution in the Netherlands'], 0,
    'The VOC originally intended only a small refreshment station to supply ships with fresh water, meat, and vegetables on the long voyage to Asia. However, the settlement expanded as free burghers began farming, leading to conflict with the Khoikhoi over land and eventually full colonisation.'),
];

