// --- Lesson 1: Causes of the French Revolution ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What is a Revolution?\n\nA **revolution** is a fundamental and dramatic change in the political system and social structure of a country, usually brought about by the people rising up against the existing government.\n\n### Characteristics of a Revolution\n\n1. It involves a **complete change** of the political system (not just a change of leader)\n2. It is usually **violent** — the old ruling class resists giving up power\n3. It is driven by **widespread popular discontent** — many ordinary people participate\n4. It produces **new ideas** about how society should be organised\n5. It has **far-reaching consequences** that extend beyond the country where it occurs\n\n### Why Study the French Revolution?\n\nThe French Revolution (1789-1799) was one of the most important events in modern history because:\n- It overthrew the **absolute monarchy** and established principles of **liberty, equality, and fraternity**\n- It inspired revolutionary movements worldwide, including in South Africa\n- It raised fundamental questions about **human rights, democracy, and social justice** that remain relevant today\n- It showed that ordinary people could challenge and overthrow even the most powerful ruling class\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| **Monarchy** | Government by a king or queen |\n| **Absolute monarchy** | The ruler has unlimited power; no parliament or constitution limits them |\n| **Ancien Regime** | The "old order" — the political and social system of France before the Revolution |\n| **Bourgeoisie** | The middle class — merchants, lawyers, bankers, professionals |\n| **Democracy** | Government by the people, usually through elected representatives |'),
  q(2, 'What is a revolution?',
    ['A fundamental change in the political system and social structure, usually through popular uprising', 'A change of king or queen through inheritance', 'A military coup by army generals', 'A gradual reform of existing laws'], 0,
    'A revolution is more than just a change of leader — it involves a complete transformation of the political system and social order. The French Revolution, for example, overthrew the absolute monarchy and the entire social hierarchy of the Ancien Regime.'),
  t(3, '## France Before the Revolution: The Ancien Regime\n\nBy 1789, France was ripe for revolution. The political, social, and economic conditions made change almost inevitable.\n\n### The Three Estates\n\nFrench society was divided into three **estates** (social classes):\n\n| Estate | Who | % of Population | Privileges |\n|--------|-----|----------------|-----------|\n| **First Estate** | Clergy (Church leaders) | Less than 1% | Owned 10% of land; paid no taxes; collected tithes (religious taxes) from peasants |\n| **Second Estate** | Nobility (aristocrats) | About 2% | Owned 25% of land; paid almost no taxes; held top government and military positions |\n| **Third Estate** | Everyone else | About 97% | Paid all the taxes; had almost no political power; included bourgeoisie, peasants, and urban workers |\n\n### The Unfair Tax System\n\n- The First and Second Estates were **exempt from most taxes**\n- The Third Estate bore almost the entire **tax burden**\n- Peasants paid: taille (land tax), gabelle (salt tax), vingtieme (income tax), tithes (to the Church), AND feudal dues (to the nobility)\n- The bourgeoisie (middle class) paid taxes but had no say in how tax money was spent\n- Tax collection was corrupt — tax collectors kept a portion for themselves'),
  fb(4, 'French society before the Revolution was divided into three ___. The First Estate (clergy) and Second Estate (nobility) made up about 3% of the population but were exempt from most ___.',
    ['estates', 'taxes'],
    'The three estates system was deeply unfair: about 97% of the population (the Third Estate) bore almost the entire tax burden while having almost no political power. The clergy and nobility, making up only about 3%, owned vast amounts of land and paid almost nothing in taxes.'),
  t(5, '## Conditions in France That Made a Revolution Probable by 1789\n\n### Political Causes\n\n1. **Absolute monarchy**: King **Louis XVI** had absolute power with no parliament or constitution to check him\n2. **Incompetent government**: Louis XVI was indecisive and weak; his wife **Marie Antoinette** was deeply unpopular (nicknamed "Madame Deficit" for her extravagant spending)\n3. **No representation**: The Estates-General (French parliament) had not been called since **1614** — the people had no voice\n\n### Economic Causes\n\n1. **Massive government debt**: France had spent heavily on wars, especially supporting the **American Revolution** (1775-1783)\n2. **Failed harvests**: The harvest of **1788** was the worst in decades, causing bread prices to soar\n3. **Bread crisis**: By 1789, ordinary Parisians were spending **80-90% of their income on bread**\n4. **Unemployment**: Economic recession left many workers jobless\n\n### Social Causes\n\n1. **Inequality**: The rigid estate system denied the Third Estate social mobility and political rights\n2. **Rising bourgeoisie**: The wealthy middle class was educated, ambitious, and frustrated by the privileges of the nobility\n3. **Peasant resentment**: Peasants worked the land but paid crushing taxes and feudal dues to lords who did nothing\n\n### Intellectual Causes: The Enlightenment\n\nNew ideas challenged the old order:\n- **Voltaire**: Attacked the power of the Church and argued for freedom of speech and religious tolerance\n- **Jean-Jacques Rousseau**: Argued that government should be based on the **"social contract"** between the ruler and the people; the people are sovereign\n- **Montesquieu**: Argued for **separation of powers** (executive, legislative, judicial) to prevent tyranny\n- The **American Revolution (1776)** proved that a people could overthrow an unjust government and create a republic based on rights'),
  q(6, 'By 1789, approximately what percentage of their income were ordinary Parisians spending on bread?',
    ['80-90%', '20-30%', '40-50%', '10-15%'], 0,
    'The bread crisis was a major trigger for the Revolution. After the disastrous harvest of 1788, the price of bread soared. Ordinary Parisians were spending 80-90% of their income just to feed themselves. When people are starving while the rich live in luxury, revolution becomes almost inevitable.'),
  t(7, '## Casting Off the Ancien Regime: Key Events of 1789\n\n### The Estates-General (May 1789)\n\n- Facing bankruptcy, Louis XVI was forced to call the **Estates-General** (French parliament) for the first time since 1614\n- The Third Estate demanded voting by **head** (one person, one vote) rather than by **estate** (which always outvoted them 2-1)\n- When the king refused, the Third Estate broke away and declared themselves the **National Assembly** (17 June 1789)\n- They took the **Tennis Court Oath** (20 June 1789): swearing not to disband until they had written a new constitution for France\n\n### The Storming of the Bastille (14 July 1789)\n\n- Rumours spread that the king was planning to use the army to crush the National Assembly\n- On **14 July 1789**, a Parisian crowd stormed the **Bastille**, a fortress-prison that symbolised royal tyranny\n- They freed the prisoners and seized weapons and gunpowder\n- The Bastille was demolished; its keys were sent to George Washington in America as a symbol of liberty\n- **14 July** is now France\'s national day (Bastille Day)\n\n### The Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen (26 August 1789)\n\nThe National Assembly adopted this historic document, declaring:\n- All men are **born and remain free and equal** in rights\n- These rights include **liberty, property, security, and resistance to oppression**\n- **Sovereignty** belongs to the nation, not the king\n- No one can be punished except under the law\n- Freedom of speech, religion, and the press'),
  fb(8, 'On 14 July 1789, a Parisian crowd stormed the ___, a fortress-prison that symbolised royal tyranny. The Declaration of the Rights of Man stated that all men are born and remain free and ___ in rights.',
    ['Bastille', 'equal'],
    'The storming of the Bastille on 14 July 1789 was the symbolic beginning of the French Revolution. The Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen established the principles of liberty and equality that would inspire democratic movements worldwide, including the anti-apartheid struggle in South Africa.'),
  t(9, '## The New Ideas: Liberty, Equality, Fraternity\n\nThe French Revolution was driven by three core ideas that became the national motto of France:\n\n### Liberty (Liberte)\n\n- Freedom from tyranny and oppressive government\n- Freedom of speech, press, and religion\n- Freedom from arbitrary arrest and imprisonment\n- The right to participate in making the laws that govern you\n\n### Equality (Egalite)\n\n- Equality before the law — no special privileges for the nobility or clergy\n- Equal taxation — everyone pays according to their ability\n- Equal opportunity — careers open to talent, not determined by birth\n- Note: "equality" in 1789 did **NOT** include women, enslaved people, or colonial subjects — this contradiction would be challenged later\n\n### Fraternity (Fraternite)\n\n- Brotherhood and solidarity among citizens\n- A sense of national community and shared purpose\n- Mutual responsibility for the welfare of all\n\n### The Contradiction\n\nThe revolutionaries proclaimed universal human rights but **did not extend them to all people**:\n- **Women** were denied political rights (Olympe de Gouges wrote the *Declaration of the Rights of Woman* in 1791 and was executed for it)\n- **Enslaved people** in French colonies were not freed until 1794 (and slavery was reimposed by Napoleon in 1802)\n- **Colonial subjects** were not included in the vision of liberty and equality\n\nThis contradiction between ideals and practice is a recurring theme in the history of rights — including in South Africa.'),
  q(10, 'What was a major contradiction in the French Revolution\'s ideas of liberty and equality?',
    ['Women, enslaved people, and colonial subjects were excluded from the rights that were proclaimed as universal', 'The ideas were only applied to the nobility', 'Liberty and equality were never formally declared', 'The Revolution immediately freed all enslaved people everywhere'], 0,
    'The French revolutionaries proclaimed universal human rights but denied them to women, enslaved people, and colonial subjects. This contradiction between ideals and practice is a pattern that recurs throughout history, including in the American "all men are created equal" while practising slavery.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Course and Impact of the French Revolution ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## The Course of the French Revolution (1789-1799)\n\n### The Constitutional Monarchy Phase (1789-1792)\n\nAfter the dramatic events of 1789, France initially tried to reform rather than abolish the monarchy:\n\n- The National Assembly abolished **feudal privileges** (4 August 1789)\n- Church lands were seized and sold to raise money (**nationalisation of Church property**)\n- A new **constitution** was adopted in 1791, creating a **constitutional monarchy** (the king kept his throne but his powers were limited by a parliament)\n- Louis XVI was forced to move from **Versailles to Paris** — effectively a prisoner of the people\n\n### The King\'s Flight and the Republic\n\n- In **June 1791**, Louis XVI and his family tried to **flee France** disguised as servants, but were caught at **Varennes** and brought back\n- This destroyed the last trust between the king and the people — he had tried to abandon his country and join foreign armies gathering to crush the Revolution\n- In **April 1792**, France went to war against Austria and Prussia, who supported the king\n- The early stages of the war went badly for France, creating a panic that "enemies within" were betraying the Revolution\n- On **10 August 1792**, a Parisian crowd stormed the **Tuileries Palace** and overthrew the monarchy\n- The **Republic** was declared on **22 September 1792**\n- Louis XVI was tried for treason, found guilty, and **executed by guillotine** on **21 January 1793**'),
  q(2, 'What happened when Louis XVI tried to flee France in June 1791?',
    ['He was caught at Varennes and brought back to Paris, destroying the last trust between king and people', 'He successfully escaped to Austria', 'He was killed during the escape attempt', 'He was immediately executed for treason'], 0,
    'The flight to Varennes was a turning point. It showed that Louis XVI was not sincere about accepting the Revolution and was hoping to join foreign armies to crush it. After this, public opinion turned decisively against the monarchy, making the king\'s trial and execution almost inevitable.'),
  t(3, '## The Reign of Terror (1793-1794)\n\nThe most radical and violent phase of the Revolution was the **Reign of Terror**, dominated by **Maximilien Robespierre** and the **Committee of Public Safety**.\n\n### Why Did the Terror Happen?\n\n1. **War**: France was at war with most of Europe (Austria, Prussia, Britain, Spain, Holland)\n2. **Civil war**: Counter-revolutionary uprisings in regions like the Vendee\n3. **Economic crisis**: Inflation, food shortages, and hoarding\n4. **Fear of conspiracy**: Paranoia about internal enemies and traitors\n\n### The Terror in Action\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Revolutionary Tribunal** | Special court that tried and sentenced "enemies of the Revolution" with minimal legal protection |\n| **The guillotine** | Adopted as the "humane" method of execution; became the symbol of the Terror |\n| **Victims** | Estimated **16,000-40,000 executed**, including Queen Marie Antoinette (16 October 1793) |\n| **Dechristianisation** | Churches closed; a new Revolutionary calendar replaced the Christian one; reason was worshipped instead of God |\n| **The Law of Suspects** | Anyone considered "suspicious" could be arrested and executed |\n\n### The Role of Ordinary People\n\nThe Revolution was not just led by politicians — ordinary people played a crucial role:\n- The **sans-culottes** (literally "without knee-breeches"): Working-class Parisians who were the street-level force of the Revolution\n- Women marched on Versailles, demanding bread\n- Neighbourhood committees enforced revolutionary laws and reported "suspects"'),
  fb(4, 'The most violent phase of the Revolution, the Reign of ___, was dominated by Maximilien Robespierre. An estimated 16,000-40,000 people were executed, many by the ___.',
    ['Terror', 'guillotine'],
    'The Reign of Terror (1793-1794) demonstrates how revolutions can devour their own children. Robespierre believed that terror was necessary to defend the Revolution from its enemies, but the mass executions eventually turned the people against him. He himself was guillotined in July 1794.'),
  t(5, '## The End of the Revolution and the Rise of Napoleon\n\n### The Fall of Robespierre (July 1794)\n\n- By mid-1794, even revolutionary leaders feared they might be next to face the guillotine\n- On **27 July 1794** (9 Thermidor in the Revolutionary calendar), Robespierre\'s opponents arrested him\n- He was **guillotined** the next day — the Revolution had consumed its own leader\n- The period after Robespierre is known as the **Thermidorian Reaction**: a move away from radicalism\n\n### Napoleon Bonaparte\n\n- **Napoleon Bonaparte**, a brilliant young military commander, rose to prominence during the revolutionary wars\n- In **November 1799**, Napoleon staged a **coup d\'etat** and became First Consul (effectively dictator)\n- In **1804**, he crowned himself **Emperor** — ending the Republic\n- Napoleon preserved some revolutionary achievements (legal equality, meritocracy, the Napoleonic Code) while abolishing others (democracy, freedom of the press)\n\n### Was the Revolution a Success or Failure?\n\n| Successes | Failures |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Destroyed the feudal system and the Ancien Regime | Did not achieve lasting democracy (Napoleon became emperor) |\n| Established principles of liberty, equality, and human rights | The Terror killed thousands of innocent people |\n| Abolished special privileges of nobility and clergy | Women\'s rights were not advanced |\n| Inspired democratic movements worldwide | Slavery in the colonies was only temporarily abolished |\n| Created a modern legal system (later through the Napoleonic Code) | Years of war and instability |'),
  q(6, 'What happened to Robespierre in July 1794?',
    ['He was arrested by his opponents and guillotined, ending the Reign of Terror', 'He voluntarily resigned from the Committee of Public Safety', 'He fled France and died in exile', 'He became the king of France'], 0,
    'Robespierre was arrested and executed by guillotine on 28 July 1794 (10 Thermidor). The irony is powerful: the man who had sent so many to the guillotine was himself consumed by the Terror he had created. His fall marked the end of the radical phase of the Revolution.'),
  t(7, '## The Impact of the French Revolution on the Rest of the World\n\nThe French Revolution\'s ideas spread far beyond France:\n\n### Haiti: The First Successful Slave Revolution\n\n- Inspired by the Declaration of the Rights of Man, enslaved people in the French colony of **Saint-Domingue** (Haiti) revolted in **1791**\n- Led by **Toussaint L\'Ouverture**, they fought against French, Spanish, and British forces\n- In **1804**, Haiti became the **first independent black republic** and the second independent nation in the Western Hemisphere\n- The Haitian Revolution proved that the ideals of liberty and equality could be claimed by those the French revolutionaries had excluded\n\n### Latin America\n\n- The revolutionary ideas inspired independence movements across Latin America in the early 19th century\n- Leaders like **Simon Bolivar** were directly influenced by the French Revolution\n\n### Impact on South Africa\n\n- The revolutionary ideas of **liberty, equality, and rights** would later influence the anti-apartheid struggle\n- The **Freedom Charter** (1955) echoed the Declaration of the Rights of Man in its demand that "The People Shall Govern"\n- The French Revolution showed that ordinary people could challenge and overthrow an unjust system — a lesson that resonated with freedom fighters everywhere\n- The concept of **human rights**, born partly from the French Revolution, became the foundation of South Africa\'s **1996 Constitution**\n\n### A Cautionary Lesson\n\nThe Revolution also offers a warning: the **Reign of Terror** shows that revolutions can become violent and destructive, and that the pursuit of a just society can sometimes lead to tyranny. This tension between revolutionary ideals and revolutionary violence is relevant to understanding liberation struggles worldwide.'),
  fb(8, 'Inspired by the French Revolution, enslaved people in the French colony of Saint-Domingue revolted in 1791. Led by Toussaint ___, Haiti became the first independent ___ republic in 1804.',
    ['L\'Ouverture', 'black'],
    'The Haitian Revolution (1791-1804) was directly inspired by the French Revolution\'s ideas of liberty and equality. It demonstrated the fundamental contradiction of the French Revolution: the same nation that proclaimed universal human rights maintained slavery in its colonies.'),
  q(9, 'How did the French Revolution influence the anti-apartheid struggle in South Africa?',
    ['Its ideas of liberty, equality, and human rights inspired the Freedom Charter and South Africa\'s democratic Constitution', 'French soldiers fought against apartheid in South Africa', 'France imposed sanctions on South Africa during apartheid', 'It had no influence on South African history'], 0,
    'The French Revolution\'s principles of liberty, equality, and human rights became foundational ideas in the global struggle for justice. The Freedom Charter (1955) and South Africa\'s 1996 Constitution reflect these principles, showing how revolutionary ideas can travel across time and space to inspire liberation movements.'),
  t(10, '## Key Terms and Concepts for Review\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| **Ancien Regime** | The old political and social system of France before 1789 |\n| **Estates** | The three social classes in pre-revolutionary France |\n| **Bourgeoisie** | The middle class (merchants, lawyers, professionals) |\n| **Sans-culottes** | Working-class Parisians; the street-level revolutionaries |\n| **Enlightenment** | Intellectual movement emphasising reason, rights, and liberty |\n| **Social contract** | Rousseau\'s idea that government authority comes from the consent of the governed |\n| **Guillotine** | Execution device that became the symbol of the Revolution |\n| **Reign of Terror** | Period of mass executions under Robespierre (1793-1794) |\n| **Republic** | A state without a king, governed by elected representatives |\n| **Coup d\'etat** | Seizure of power by force (Napoleon, 1799) |\n\n### Source-Based Skills: Analysing Revolution\n\nWhen analysing sources about the French Revolution, consider:\n1. **Who created the source?** (A noble? A sans-culotte? A revolutionary leader?)\n2. **When was it created?** (Before, during, or after the Revolution?)\n3. **What is the purpose?** (Propaganda? Personal record? Official document?)\n4. **What perspective does it represent?** (Is it biased towards or against the Revolution?)\n5. **How does it compare with other sources?** (Do different sources agree or contradict each other?)'),
];

