// --- Lesson 1: Britain Takes Control of the Cape ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## How Did Colonial Expansion Into the Interior Transform South Africa?\n\nTerm 3 focuses on how British colonial expansion into the South African interior, combined with the resistance and responses of indigenous peoples and Boer settlers, transformed the region.\n\n### Britain Takes Control of the Cape\n\nThe British took control of the Cape Colony in two stages:\n- **1795**: Britain seized the Cape from the Dutch during the French Revolutionary Wars (to prevent France, which had conquered the Netherlands, from controlling the strategic route to India)\n- **1803**: The Cape was briefly returned to the Dutch (Batavian Republic)\n- **1806**: Britain seized the Cape again permanently during the Napoleonic Wars\n- **1814**: The Treaty of Vienna formally ceded the Cape to Britain\n\n### Why Did Britain Want the Cape?\n\n1. **Strategic position**: The Cape controlled the sea route between Europe and Asia (especially India)\n2. **Naval power**: Britain needed harbours for its navy to protect trade routes\n3. **Preventing French control**: During the Napoleonic Wars, Britain could not allow France to control the Cape\n\n### Early British Changes at the Cape\n\n| Reform | Effect |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Circuit Courts** | Travelling courts that allowed Khoikhoi servants to bring complaints against Boer masters |\n| **Ordinance 50 (1828)** | Gave Khoikhoi and other free people of colour legal equality with whites |\n| **English as official language** (1828) | Replaced Dutch as the language of government and courts |\n| **1820 Settlers** | 4,000 British settlers brought to the Eastern Cape to strengthen the frontier |'),
  q(2, 'Why did Britain seize control of the Cape Colony in 1795?',
    ['To prevent France, which had conquered the Netherlands, from controlling the strategic sea route to India', 'To find gold and diamonds', 'To spread Christianity to indigenous peoples', 'To establish a penal colony like Australia'], 0,
    'Britain seized the Cape primarily for strategic reasons. The Cape controlled the sea route between Europe and Asia, and Britain could not allow its rival France (which had conquered the Netherlands) to control this vital position. British commercial interests in India made the Cape essential.'),
  t(3, '## Indigenous Peoples: Driven Out or Drawn Into the Labour Force\n\nBritish colonial expansion had devastating consequences for indigenous peoples.\n\n### The Khoikhoi and San\n\n- By 1750, the **Khoikhoi** had already lost most of their land and cattle to Dutch settlers\n- Many Khoikhoi had been reduced to **servants (labourers)** on Boer farms\n- The **San** (hunter-gatherers) were actively hunted and killed by settlers who saw them as threats to their livestock\n- **Commando raids** against the San amounted to genocide in some regions\n- By the early 19th century, the Khoikhoi and San had lost their independent way of life and were incorporated into the colonial labour force as servants, farm workers, and domestic labourers\n\n### Changing Labour Patterns\n\n- **1807**: Britain abolished the **slave trade** (the buying and selling of enslaved people across the Atlantic)\n- **1834**: Britain abolished **slavery** throughout the British Empire (enacted at the Cape on 1 December 1834)\n- However, abolition did NOT mean freedom in practice:\n  - Former enslaved people were forced to serve a 4-year **"apprenticeship"** period (until 1838)\n  - **Masters and Servants laws** controlled labour through contracts that were heavily weighted in favour of employers\n  - Pass laws and vagrancy laws restricted the movement of black workers\n  - The colonial economy remained dependent on cheap, controlled, non-white labour\n\n### The Irony\n\nWhile slavery was formally abolished, the colonial labour system that replaced it was designed to keep indigenous peoples and former enslaved people as a **cheap labour force** for white farmers and businesses. The patterns established in this period — pass laws, labour controls, racial discrimination — would later form the foundations of apartheid.'),
  fb(4, 'Britain abolished the slave trade in ___ and slavery throughout the British Empire in 1834. However, former enslaved people at the Cape were forced to serve a 4-year "___" period before being fully free.',
    ['1807', 'apprenticeship'],
    'The abolition of slavery was a major achievement, but it did not bring genuine freedom. The apprenticeship system, combined with Masters and Servants laws and pass laws, ensured that former enslaved people and indigenous workers remained a cheap, controlled labour force. The transition from slavery to other forms of labour exploitation is a key theme in South African history.'),
  t(5, '## Expanding Frontiers and Trade\n\nThe British Cape Colony expanded its frontiers throughout the early 19th century, bringing it into conflict with indigenous peoples.\n\n### The Eastern Frontier: Cape-Xhosa Wars\n\n- The **eastern frontier** was a zone of conflict between the expanding Cape Colony and the **Xhosa** kingdoms\n- Nine **frontier wars** (also called the **Cape-Xhosa Wars** or Xhosa Wars of Resistance) were fought between **1779 and 1879** — the longest series of colonial wars in African history\n- Each war resulted in the Xhosa losing more land\n- The British used a combination of military force and diplomacy to push the frontier eastward\n\n### The Northern Frontier\n\n- Boer **trekboers** (migrating farmers) pushed northward and eastward into Khoikhoi, San, and Tswana territories\n- **Griqua** communities (people of mixed Khoikhoi, European, and enslaved heritage) established independent states on the northern frontier\n- Trade in ivory, cattle, hides, and firearms connected frontier communities\n\n### Boer Response to British Control: Trekking Into the Interior\n\nMany **Boers (Afrikaners)** were unhappy with British rule because:\n1. **Language**: English replaced Dutch; Boers felt culturally marginalised\n2. **Abolition of slavery**: Boers relied on enslaved labour for their farms\n3. **Equal treatment**: Ordinance 50 (1828) gave Khoikhoi legal equality — Boers resented this\n4. **Inadequate compensation**: Boers felt they were not properly compensated for freed enslaved people\n5. **Frontier policy**: The British government tried to control frontier conflict, frustrating Boer expansion\n\nFrom **1835 onwards**, approximately **12,000-14,000 Boers** left the Cape Colony in the **Great Trek**, migrating into the interior to establish independent republics. This movement would have enormous consequences for the peoples already living in those territories.'),
  q(6, 'Why did many Boers leave the Cape Colony during the Great Trek (from 1835)?',
    ['They were unhappy with British rule, especially the abolition of slavery, Ordinance 50, and the imposition of English', 'They wanted to find gold in the interior', 'They were forced to leave by the British government', 'They wanted to join the Zulu kingdom'], 0,
    'The Boers left the Cape because of multiple grievances with British rule: the abolition of slavery (which undermined their farming economy), Ordinance 50 (which gave Khoikhoi legal equality), the imposition of English, and British frontier policies. They sought to establish their own independent republics in the interior.'),
  t(7, '## Xhosa Responses: Cooperation and Conflict, Including the Cattle Killing\n\nThe Xhosa people responded to colonial expansion in various ways, ranging from military resistance to cooperation to a devastating prophetic movement.\n\n### Resistance\n\n- The Xhosa fought nine frontier wars over a century, demonstrating determined resistance to colonisation\n- Leaders like **Hintsa**, **Sandile**, and **Maqoma** led military campaigns against the British\n- The Xhosa adapted their tactics, sometimes acquiring firearms through trade\n\n### The Cattle Killing of 1856-1857\n\nOne of the most tragic events in Xhosa history occurred when a young prophetess named **Nongqawuse** had a vision:\n\n- She claimed that the **ancestors** had told her that if the Xhosa killed all their cattle and destroyed their crops, the ancestors would return, drive the Europeans into the sea, and bring fresh, healthy cattle\n- The prophecy occurred during a devastating **lung sickness (bovine pleuropneumonia)** that was killing Xhosa cattle\n- **Chief Sarhili** and many Xhosa believed the prophecy and began killing their cattle and destroying their grain stores\n- Between **February 1856 and June 1857**, an estimated **400,000 cattle** were killed\n- The result was catastrophic **famine**: approximately **40,000 Xhosa died** of starvation and an estimated **33,000 were forced to seek work** in the Cape Colony\n- The Xhosa population dropped from about **105,000 to 37,000**\n\n### Why Did the Cattle Killing Happen?\n\n- **Desperation**: After decades of war and land loss, the Xhosa were desperate for a solution\n- **Disease**: The cattle lung sickness was destroying their herds anyway\n- **Spiritual belief**: The ancestors were a powerful spiritual authority in Xhosa culture\n- **Colonial pressure**: The cumulative impact of colonial expansion had created unbearable conditions'),
  fb(8, 'The young Xhosa prophetess ___ predicted that if the Xhosa killed their cattle, the ancestors would drive the Europeans into the sea. The resulting famine killed approximately ___ Xhosa people.',
    ['Nongqawuse', '40,000'],
    'The Cattle Killing of 1856-1857 was one of the greatest tragedies in South African history. It must be understood in context: the Xhosa were desperate after decades of colonial wars and land loss, and a devastating cattle disease was already killing their herds. The catastrophe permanently weakened Xhosa resistance and provided the colonial government with a cheap labour force.'),
  t(9, '## The Zulu Kingdom and the Colony of Natal\n\nThe British colony of **Natal** (established 1843) brought colonial rule to the eastern seaboard and created new conflicts with the Zulu kingdom.\n\n### Indentured Indian Labourers\n\n- The Natal sugar plantations needed a large, cheap labour force\n- Local Zulu and other African people refused to work on sugar plantations for low wages\n- From **1860**, the British imported **indentured Indian labourers** from India\n- Between 1860 and 1911, approximately **152,000 Indians** came to Natal\n- They signed contracts to work for 5 years at low wages in harsh conditions\n- After their contracts ended, many stayed in South Africa, forming the foundation of South Africa\'s Indian community\n- Indian workers also built railways and worked in coal mines\n\n### The Anglo-Zulu War of 1879\n\n- In **1879**, the British invaded the Zulu kingdom to eliminate an independent African power on their border\n- **Battle of Isandlwana** (22 January 1879): The Zulu army inflicted a devastating defeat on the British, killing approximately **1,300 British soldiers** — the worst British defeat by an indigenous African force\n- **Battle of Rorke\'s Drift** (22-23 January 1879): A small British garrison defended against a Zulu attack\n- **Battle of Ulundi** (4 July 1879): The British finally defeated the Zulu army using superior firepower\n- The Zulu kingdom was **divided into 13 chieftainships** under British supervision, ending its independence'),
  q(10, 'What happened at the Battle of Isandlwana in January 1879?',
    ['The Zulu army inflicted a devastating defeat on the British, killing approximately 1,300 soldiers', 'The British easily defeated the Zulu army', 'The Zulu surrendered without fighting', 'Both sides agreed to a peace treaty'], 0,
    'The Battle of Isandlwana (22 January 1879) was the worst defeat the British army ever suffered at the hands of an indigenous African force. The Zulu warriors, using their traditional tactics and superior numbers, overwhelmed a British column despite the British having firearms and artillery. This battle showed the military capability of the Zulu kingdom.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The Highveld and Boer Republics ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Cooperation and Conflict on the Highveld\n\nThe Great Trek brought Boer settlers into the interior, where they encountered African kingdoms and communities. The relationships between these groups were characterised by both cooperation and conflict.\n\n### The Boer Republics\n\nThe voortrekkers established two major independent republics:\n\n| Republic | Established | Capital | Key Features |\n|----------|------------|---------|-------------|\n| **South African Republic (ZAR / Transvaal)** | 1852 (Sand River Convention) | Pretoria | Largest Boer republic; recognised by Britain |\n| **Orange Free State (OFS)** | 1854 (Bloemfontein Convention) | Bloemfontein | Smaller but more stable; recognised by Britain |\n\n### Boer Society\n\n- The Boers were deeply religious **Calvinists** who believed they were chosen by God (like the Israelites) to settle the land\n- They practised **subsistence farming** — large farms (6,000 acres was common) with cattle, sheep, and grain\n- They relied on **African labour** — despite leaving the Cape partly because of abolition, they used various systems of forced or coerced labour\n- They had **no franchise (voting rights)** for black people — their constitutions explicitly stated that there would be "no equality in church or state" between white and black\n- Government was **decentralised**: the Volksraad (parliament) had authority, but individual farmers were largely independent'),
  q(2, 'What did the constitutions of the Boer republics say about racial equality?',
    ['They explicitly stated there would be "no equality in church or state" between white and black', 'They guaranteed equal rights for all races', 'They did not mention race at all', 'They gave limited voting rights to educated black people'], 0,
    'The Boer republican constitutions were explicitly racist, stating that there would be "no equality in church or state." This meant that black Africans had no political rights, could not own land, and were subject to forced labour systems. This principle of racial inequality would later be formalised in apartheid.'),
  t(3, '## The Basotho Kingdom Under Moshoeshoe: A Case Study\n\nThe interaction between the **Basotho kingdom** under **Moshoeshoe** and the **Orange Free State** is a key case study of cooperation and conflict on the highveld.\n\n### Sources of Conflict\n\n1. **Land**: The Free State and the Basotho both claimed the fertile land of the **Caledon River valley**\n2. **Cattle raiding**: Both sides raided each other\'s cattle, escalating tensions\n3. **Expanding settlement**: Free State farmers pushed into Basotho territory\n\n### The Basotho-Free State Wars\n\n| War | Date | Outcome |\n|-----|------|---------|\n| **First War** | 1858 | Free State attacked but failed to take Thaba Bosiu; peace treaty but land disputes continued |\n| **Second War** | 1865-1868 | Free State seized large areas of fertile Basotho land; Moshoeshoe faced starvation and defeat |\n\n### British Protection\n\n- Facing destruction by the Free State, Moshoeshoe appealed to the **British** for protection\n- In **1868**, Britain declared Basutoland (modern Lesotho) a British protectorate\n- This saved the Basotho from complete conquest by the Free State but placed them under colonial rule\n- Moshoeshoe died in **1870**, having preserved his people through a combination of military defence, diplomacy, and strategic alliances\n\n### Moshoeshoe\'s Achievement\n\nMoshoeshoe\'s greatest achievement was preserving the independence of his people during a period when virtually every other African kingdom in southern Africa was being conquered. Lesotho remains an independent nation today, surrounded by South Africa — a direct result of Moshoeshoe\'s skilful leadership.'),
  fb(4, 'Facing defeat by the Orange Free State, Moshoeshoe appealed to the British for protection in ___. His territory became the British protectorate of Basutoland, which is today the independent nation of ___.',
    ['1868', 'Lesotho'],
    'Moshoeshoe\'s decision to seek British protection in 1868 was a brilliant strategic move. Rather than face complete conquest by the Free State, he accepted British colonial authority, which at least preserved his people\'s territorial integrity. Lesotho\'s independence today is a direct result of this decision.'),
  t(5, '## Background to the South African War: Mining Capitalism\n\nThe discovery of **diamonds** (1867) and **gold** (1886) in southern Africa transformed the region and set the stage for the South African War (1899-1902).\n\n### Diamonds at Kimberley (1867)\n\n- Diamonds were discovered near the **Vaal River** in 1867\n- The town of **Kimberley** grew rapidly as prospectors arrived from around the world\n- Britain annexed the diamond fields (Griqualand West) in 1871, despite rival claims from the Orange Free State and the Griqua\n- By the 1880s, diamond mining was dominated by large companies, especially **De Beers**, founded by **Cecil John Rhodes**\n\n### Gold on the Witwatersrand (1886)\n\n- Gold was discovered on the **Witwatersrand** (near modern Johannesburg) in **1886**\n- The Witwatersrand goldfields proved to be the largest gold deposit in the world\n- The mining town of **Johannesburg** grew explosively — from nothing in 1886 to the largest city in southern Africa within a decade\n- Unlike alluvial gold, Witwatersrand gold was deep underground and required **massive capital investment** in mining machinery, railways, and chemicals\n\n### The Influx of Capital\n\n- British and international **mining magnates** (known as **Randlords**) invested heavily\n- Key figures: Cecil Rhodes, Alfred Beit, Barney Barnato, Julius Wernher\n- Mining companies were listed on the **Johannesburg Stock Exchange** (founded 1887)\n- The mining industry attracted thousands of workers, businesses, and professionals, creating a cosmopolitan city within the conservative Boer republic'),
  q(6, 'Why was the discovery of gold on the Witwatersrand in 1886 different from other gold discoveries?',
    ['The gold was deep underground and required massive capital investment in machinery, railways, and chemicals', 'It was the only gold discovery in African history', 'The gold was easy to access and could be mined by individuals', 'It was immediately controlled by the Boer government'], 0,
    'Witwatersrand gold was embedded deep in hard rock, unlike alluvial gold that could be panned from rivers. Mining it required expensive equipment, deep shafts, chemical processing, and large workforces. This meant only well-capitalised companies could mine profitably, leading to the concentration of wealth in the hands of a few mining magnates.'),
  t(7, '## The Creation of a Racially Divided Labour Force\n\nThe mining industry created a **racially divided labour system** that would shape South Africa for the next century.\n\n### The Mining Labour System\n\n| Feature | White Workers | Black Workers |\n|---------|--------------|---------------|\n| **Jobs** | Skilled and supervisory positions | Unskilled manual labour (drilling, loading rock) |\n| **Wages** | 10-15 times higher than black wages | Extremely low; barely enough to survive |\n| **Contracts** | Free to change jobs | Bound by contracts; migrant labour system |\n| **Living conditions** | Lived with families in town | Housed in single-sex **compounds** (hostels) near mines |\n| **Legal status** | Full rights; could vote (in Boer republics) | No political rights; controlled by pass laws |\n\n### The Migrant Labour System\n\n- Black workers came from across southern Africa (Mozambique, Lesotho, Swaziland, and rural South Africa) on **short-term contracts** (usually 6-12 months)\n- They left their families in rural areas and lived in **compounds** (fenced hostels) controlled by the mining companies\n- After their contracts ended, they were forced to return home\n- This system provided mines with a cheap, controlled labour force while preventing black urbanisation\n\n### Legislation of Job Reservation\n\n- The **Mines and Works Act (1911)** reserved skilled jobs for white workers (the **colour bar**)\n- White workers earned much higher wages than black workers for the same hours\n- Black workers were denied training and promotion\n- This created **structural insecurity for white workers** — they feared that mine owners might replace them with cheaper black labour, which bred racism\n\n### Impact on South Africa\n\nThe mining labour system created patterns that would define South Africa:\n- **Racial wage gap**: The principle that white workers deserved higher wages than black workers\n- **Migrant labour**: The separation of black workers from their families\n- **Compounds**: The forerunners of apartheid-era hostels\n- **Job reservation**: The colour bar that protected white workers at the expense of black workers'),
  fb(8, 'The ___ and Works Act of 1911 reserved skilled mining jobs for white workers, creating the colour bar. Black workers lived in single-sex ___ (hostels) controlled by the mining companies.',
    ['Mines', 'compounds'],
    'The mining compound system and the colour bar were foundational to South Africa\'s racial labour system. They established the principle that black workers would be cheap, controlled, and temporary — patterns that were later formalised and extended by apartheid legislation.'),
  t(9, '## Britain\'s Increasing Interest and the Road to War\n\nThe discovery of gold intensified British interest in controlling the Transvaal (South African Republic).\n\n### Cecil Rhodes and the Jameson Raid\n\n- **Cecil John Rhodes** — diamond magnate, prime minister of the Cape Colony, and arch-imperialist — wanted British control over all of southern Africa\n- The **Uitlanders** (foreigners, mainly British) in Johannesburg had no voting rights in the Transvaal and resented Boer President **Paul Kruger\'s** government\n- In **December 1895**, Rhodes organised the **Jameson Raid** — an armed incursion into the Transvaal intended to trigger an Uitlander uprising and overthrow Kruger\'s government\n- The Raid was a **disastrous failure**: Jameson was captured, Rhodes was disgraced and forced to resign as Cape Prime Minister\n- However, the Raid increased tensions between Britain and the Transvaal\n\n### Political and Economic Struggle for Control\n\nBy the late 1890s, conflict seemed inevitable:\n\n| British Interests | Boer Interests |\n|-------------------|----------------|\n| Control of the world\'s richest goldfields | Maintain independence of Boer republics |\n| Protect Uitlander rights (and British investment) | Resist British imperialism and interference |\n| Create a united, British-controlled southern Africa | Preserve Afrikaner culture and way of life |\n| Strategic control of routes to India | Control their own economy and labour |\n\n- Negotiations between British High Commissioner **Sir Alfred Milner** and President Kruger failed\n- Milner deliberately pushed for war, believing it would be quick and easy\n- In **October 1899**, the Boer republics issued an ultimatum to Britain, and the **South African War** began'),
  q(10, 'What was the Jameson Raid and why did it fail?',
    ['An armed incursion into the Transvaal organised by Cecil Rhodes to overthrow Kruger, which failed because the expected Uitlander uprising never happened', 'A Boer attack on the Cape Colony that was defeated by the British', 'A naval battle between Britain and the Transvaal', 'A diplomatic mission by Jameson to negotiate peace with Kruger'], 0,
    'The Jameson Raid (December 1895) was a botched attempt by Cecil Rhodes to overthrow the Transvaal government. Dr Leander Starr Jameson led about 500 men into the Transvaal expecting the Uitlanders in Johannesburg to rise up, but they did not. The raiders were captured, and Rhodes was forced to resign in disgrace.'),
];

