// --- Lesson 1: The South African War 1899-1902 ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The South African War 1899-1902\n\nThe **South African War** (also known as the Second Anglo-Boer War or the Boer War) was fought between the **British Empire** and the two **Boer republics** (the Transvaal/ZAR and the Orange Free State) from **October 1899 to May 1902**.\n\n### South Africa on the Eve of War\n\nBy 1899, southern Africa was deeply divided:\n\n| Territory | Government | Population |\n|-----------|------------|------------|\n| **Cape Colony** | British colony with limited self-government | Multi-racial; significant Afrikaner population |\n| **Natal** | British colony | British settlers, Zulu, Indian labourers |\n| **Transvaal (ZAR)** | Independent Boer republic under Paul Kruger | Boers, Uitlanders, black Africans |\n| **Orange Free State** | Independent Boer republic | Boers, black Africans |\n\n### Phases of the War\n\n**Phase 1: Boer Offensive (October-December 1899)**\n- The Boers struck first, invading Natal and the Cape Colony\n- They besieged three British towns: **Ladysmith**, **Mafeking**, and **Kimberley**\n- The British suffered defeats at Stormberg, Magersfontein, and Colenso — a period called **"Black Week"** (December 1899)\n- The Boers were skilled horsemen and marksmen, using guerrilla-style tactics and knowledge of the terrain\n\n**Phase 2: British Counter-Offensive (February-September 1900)**\n- Britain sent massive reinforcements under **Lord Roberts** and **Lord Kitchener** (eventually 450,000 troops)\n- The sieges were lifted; British forces captured Bloemfontein (March), Johannesburg (May), and Pretoria (June 1900)\n- Roberts declared the war "virtually over" and returned to Britain — prematurely\n\n**Phase 3: Guerrilla War (September 1900-May 1902)**\n- Boer commandos under leaders like **Louis Botha**, **Jan Smuts**, **Christiaan de Wet**, and **Koos de la Rey** fought a guerrilla war\n- Small, mobile Boer units attacked British supply lines, railways, and isolated garrisons\n- The British could not defeat the guerrillas using conventional military tactics'),
  q(2, 'What was "Black Week" in December 1899?',
    ['A period when the British army suffered three major defeats (Stormberg, Magersfontein, Colenso) against the Boers', 'A week of peace negotiations between Britain and the Boer republics', 'A week of celebrations after the British captured Pretoria', 'A week of intense fighting in the siege of Mafeking'], 0,
    'Black Week (10-15 December 1899) was a humiliating period for the British army, suffering three significant defeats in one week. It shocked the British public, who had expected a quick victory over what they saw as a small group of farmers. Britain was forced to send massive reinforcements.'),
  t(3, '## Britain\'s Response: Scorched Earth and Concentration Camps\n\nTo defeat the Boer guerrillas, the British under **Lord Kitchener** adopted brutal tactics:\n\n### Scorched Earth Policy\n\n- British forces systematically **burned Boer farms**, destroyed crops, poisoned wells, and slaughtered livestock\n- The aim was to deny guerrillas food, shelter, and support from the civilian population\n- Over **30,000 Boer farmhouses** were destroyed\n- The countryside was devastated, creating widespread famine\n\n### Concentration Camps\n\nTo control the civilian population, the British established **concentration camps** for Boer women and children (and separate camps for black Africans):\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Number of camps** | 45 camps for Boers; 64 camps for black Africans |\n| **Population** | Approximately 116,000 Boer civilians; 115,000 black Africans |\n| **Conditions** | Overcrowded; inadequate food, water, and sanitation; minimal medical care |\n| **Disease** | Measles, typhoid, dysentery, and pneumonia killed thousands |\n| **Deaths (Boer)** | Approximately **26,000 Boer women and children** died (plus about 1,700 Boer men) |\n| **Deaths (Black)** | At least **14,000 black Africans** died (likely an undercount due to poor record-keeping) |\n\n### Emily Hobhouse\n\n- **Emily Hobhouse**, a British welfare campaigner, visited the concentration camps in 1901 and was **horrified** by the conditions\n- She publicised the suffering in Britain, causing a public outcry\n- The **Fawcett Commission** was sent to investigate and recommended improvements\n- Hobhouse is remembered as a hero in South Africa for her courage in exposing the truth'),
  fb(4, 'Under Kitchener\'s scorched ___ policy, over 30,000 Boer farmhouses were destroyed. Approximately 26,000 ___ women and children died in British concentration camps.',
    ['earth', 'Boer'],
    'Kitchener\'s scorched earth policy and concentration camps were among the most controversial aspects of the war. The death of 26,000 Boer women and children (mostly from disease) created deep bitterness that shaped Afrikaner nationalism for generations. Emily Hobhouse\'s exposure of the camps was an important act of conscience.'),
  t(5, '## The Role and Experiences of Women in the War\n\nWomen played important roles on all sides of the South African War.\n\n### Boer Women\n\n- Many Boer women **managed farms** alone while their husbands fought\n- Some actively supported the commandos by providing food, hiding fighters, and passing messages\n- A few women, like **Hendrina Joubert**, accompanied the commandos\n- The majority of concentration camp victims were women and children — making them the primary victims of the war\n- The suffering of Boer women in the camps became central to **Afrikaner nationalist memory** — it was used for decades to build a sense of Afrikaner victimhood and anti-British sentiment\n\n### Black Women\n\n- Black women\'s experiences were largely **ignored** in historical accounts until recently\n- Many black women worked on Boer farms and were displaced by the scorched earth policy\n- They were placed in **separate concentration camps** with even worse conditions than the Boer camps\n- At least **14,000 black people** died in these camps, but records were poorly kept\n- Their suffering was overlooked because the dominant historical narrative focused on white experiences\n\n### British Women\n\n- **Emily Hobhouse** (1860-1926) was the most famous British woman associated with the war\n- She travelled to South Africa at her own expense to investigate conditions in the camps\n- Her reports shocked the British public and pressured the government to improve camp conditions\n- Other British women served as nurses, both in military hospitals and in the camps'),
  q(6, 'Why were the experiences of black women in the South African War largely ignored?',
    ['The dominant historical narrative focused on white experiences, and records for black camps were poorly kept', 'Black women were not affected by the war', 'Black women\'s experiences were fully documented at the time', 'Black women supported the British and were treated well'], 0,
    'The marginalisation of black experiences is a key historiographical issue. At least 14,000 black people died in concentration camps, but their suffering was largely erased from the historical record. Afrikaner and British narratives dominated, treating the war as a "white man\'s war" when in reality it affected all South Africans profoundly.'),
  t(7, '## The Role and Experiences of Black South Africans in the War\n\nThe South African War was not just a "white man\'s war" — black South Africans were deeply affected and actively involved.\n\n### Black Participation\n\n- Both sides used **black labourers** for tasks like digging trenches, building fortifications, driving wagons, and caring for horses\n- An estimated **100,000 black people** served on the British side as labourers, scouts, messengers, and guards\n- Some black men **armed themselves** and fought, although both sides officially claimed this was a "white man\'s war"\n- The **Kgatla** people in the Transvaal used the war as an opportunity to reclaim land and cattle seized by the Boers\n\n### Black Expectations\n\n- Many black South Africans supported the British, hoping that a British victory would bring them:\n  - **Political rights** (the franchise)\n  - **Land reform** (return of seized land)\n  - **Equal treatment** before the law\n  - An end to the racial policies of the Boer republics\n\n### The Betrayal\n\n- After the British victory, **none of these expectations were met**\n- The **Treaty of Vereeniging** (31 May 1902), which ended the war, left the question of black political rights to be decided by the future self-governing colonies — effectively ensuring that blacks would not get the vote\n- Britain **sacrificed black rights** to achieve reconciliation with the Boers\n- This betrayal would be confirmed by the **South Africa Act of 1909**, which created the Union of South Africa with a franchise limited to white men (except for a limited non-white franchise in the Cape)\n\n### The Union of South Africa (1910)\n\n- On **31 May 1910**, the four colonies (Cape, Natal, Transvaal, Orange River Colony) were united as the **Union of South Africa**\n- The constitution denied political rights to the vast majority of the population\n- This set the stage for the racial inequality that would culminate in apartheid in 1948'),
  fb(8, 'The Treaty of ___ (31 May 1902) ended the South African War. Britain sacrificed black rights to achieve reconciliation with the Boers, and the Union of South Africa was created on 31 May ___.',
    ['Vereeniging', '1910'],
    'The Treaty of Vereeniging and the creation of the Union of South Africa represented a profound betrayal of black South Africans. Despite their contributions to the British war effort and their hopes for equal rights, black people were excluded from political power in the new state. This betrayal laid the foundations for apartheid.'),
  t(9, '## End of the War: Peace Negotiations\n\nThe war ended through negotiations that reflected the power dynamics and priorities of the time.\n\n### Why Did the War End?\n\n1. **Boer exhaustion**: After nearly three years of guerrilla warfare, the Boer commandos were running out of supplies and manpower\n2. **Concentration camps**: The suffering of Boer women and children pressured Boer leaders to negotiate\n3. **British frustration**: The war was costing Britain enormously in money and reputation\n4. **International criticism**: The concentration camps drew worldwide condemnation\n\n### The Treaty of Vereeniging (31 May 1902)\n\nKey terms:\n- The Boer republics **surrendered their independence** and became British colonies\n- The Boers were promised **responsible self-government** in the future\n- £3 million was provided for **reconstruction** of the devastated countryside\n- **Boers could keep their firearms** for self-defence\n- Dutch would be taught in schools and used in courts where necessary\n- The **franchise for "natives"** would not be decided until after self-government was granted — effectively a promise to deny black political rights\n\n### Consequences of the War\n\n| Consequence | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| **Boer suffering** | 26,000 women and children died; farms destroyed; deep bitterness towards Britain |\n| **Black betrayal** | Despite supporting Britain, black South Africans gained nothing |\n| **Union of South Africa** | Created in 1910 with a whites-only franchise (except Cape) |\n| **Afrikaner nationalism** | The suffering of the war fuelled Afrikaner nationalism, which culminated in the NP victory of 1948 |\n| **Mining capitalism** | The gold mines continued under British-friendly policies |\n| **Modern South Africa** | The war and Union set the stage for the racial conflicts of the 20th century |'),
  q(10, 'Why was the Treaty of Vereeniging a betrayal of black South Africans?',
    ['It left the question of black political rights to the future self-governing colonies, ensuring blacks would be excluded from power', 'It gave black South Africans equal voting rights', 'It returned all African land that had been seized', 'It created a non-racial democracy in South Africa'], 0,
    'The Treaty of Vereeniging\'s most damaging clause was that the franchise for "natives" would not be decided until after self-government was granted. This effectively guaranteed that the white-ruled self-governing colonies would deny black people the vote. Britain chose Boer reconciliation over black justice.'),
];

