blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## The Native Land Act of 1913\n\nThe **Natives\' Land Act, No. 27 of 1913** was one of the most devastating pieces of legislation in South African history. Signed into law on **19 June 1913**, it formalised the dispossession of African peoples that had been occurring for centuries.\n\n### What Did the Act Do?\n\n1. It **prohibited Africans from owning or renting land** outside designated "native reserves"\n2. The reserves comprised only **7.3%** of South Africa\'s total land area (later increased to 13.6% by the 1936 Native Trust and Land Act)\n3. It **outlawed sharecropping** — the practice where African farmers shared a portion of their crop with a white landowner in return for use of the land\n4. It made it **illegal for Africans to buy land** from white people outside the reserves\n5. Africans living on white-owned land could only remain as **labour tenants** (working for the landowner in exchange for the right to live on the land)\n\n### The Immediate Impact\n\nThe Act was implemented on **19 June 1913** — overnight, millions of Africans became illegal occupants of the land they had farmed for generations:\n\n- **Sharecroppers** were given the choice: become labour tenants (virtual slaves) or leave the land\n- Thousands of families were **evicted** from farms, forced to wander with their livestock looking for a place to live\n- Families who refused to become labour tenants were loaded onto wagons and dumped on the roadside\n- The reserves were overcrowded, with insufficient land, water, and resources for the people forced into them'),
  q(2, 'What did the Natives\' Land Act of 1913 restrict African land ownership to?',
    ['7.3% of South Africa\'s total land area', '50% of the land', '25% of the land', '13% of the land from the beginning'], 0,
    'The 1913 Land Act restricted African land ownership to just 7.3% of South Africa\'s land — for a population that comprised the overwhelming majority of the country. The 1936 Native Trust and Land Act later increased this to 13%, but this was still grossly inadequate. Africans comprised about 67% of the population but were confined to about 13% of the land.'),
  t(3, '## Sol Plaatje and the Economic and Social Impact\n\n### Sol Plaatje: Witness to Injustice\n\n**Solomon Tshekisho Plaatje** (1876-1932) was a journalist, writer, linguist, and political activist who became the first Secretary-General of the SANNC (later ANC). He is the most important witness to the impact of the 1913 Land Act.\n\n- After the Act was passed, Plaatje **travelled through the Orange Free State** documenting its effects on African families\n- He published his findings in **Native Life in South Africa** (1916), one of the most powerful works of South African non-fiction\n\n### Plaatje\'s Account\n\nPlaatje wrote: *"Awakening on Friday morning, June 20, 1913, the South African native found himself, not actually a slave, but a pariah in the land of his birth."*\n\nHe documented:\n- Families wandering the countryside with their livestock, turned away by every farmer\n- Children dying of cold and exposure on the roadside\n- Livestock dying because there was no grazing land\n- Farmers who had been independent sharecroppers being forced into labour tenancy\n- The **utter devastation** of communities that had been stable and productive\n\n### Economic Impact\n\n| On Africans | On White Farmers |\n|-------------|------------------|\n| Lost land ownership and farming independence | Gained cheap, controlled labour force |\n| Forced into reserves or labour tenancy | Consolidated control over agricultural land |\n| Reduced to wage labourers on mines and farms | Reduced labour costs through elimination of sharecropping |\n| Overcrowding in reserves led to soil erosion and poverty | Benefited from state support for white agriculture |\n| Created migrant labour system (men working in cities, families in reserves) | Built wealth on the foundation of dispossessed African labour |'),
  fb(4, 'Sol ___ documented the impact of the 1913 Land Act in his book Native Life in South Africa. He wrote that the South African native found himself "not actually a slave, but a ___ in the land of his birth."',
    ['Plaatje', 'pariah'],
    'Plaatje\'s Native Life in South Africa (1916) is one of the most important works of South African literature and political writing. His first-hand documentation of the suffering caused by the Land Act preserved the voices and experiences of those who were dispossessed.'),
  t(5, '## Social Impact of the Land Act\n\nThe 1913 Land Act had profound social consequences that reshaped South African society.\n\n### Breakdown of Family Life\n\n- Men were forced to leave their families in overcrowded reserves and migrate to **mines and cities** for work\n- The **migrant labour system** separated families for months or years at a time\n- Women, children, and the elderly were left in the reserves to survive on subsistence farming on inadequate land\n- Traditional social structures were undermined as communities were fragmented\n\n### Poverty and Overcrowding\n\n- The reserves were far too small for the number of people forced into them\n- **Soil erosion** accelerated as too many people farmed too little land\n- Malnutrition, disease, and infant mortality rates were far higher in the reserves than in white areas\n- The reserves became reservoirs of cheap labour for the mining and farming industries\n\n### Urbanisation\n\n- Despite laws designed to keep Africans in the reserves, many moved to cities in search of work\n- They lived in **townships** on the outskirts of cities, in overcrowded and under-serviced conditions\n- Urban Africans faced pass laws, curfews, and the constant threat of deportation back to the reserves\n\n### The SANNC Response\n\n- The **SANNC (later ANC)** sent a **delegation to London** in 1914 to protest the Land Act, but they were ignored by the British government (which was focused on the outbreak of World War I)\n- This early failure of moderate, constitutional protest foreshadowed the frustrations that would eventually lead to mass action and armed resistance decades later'),
  q(6, 'What happened when the SANNC sent a delegation to London in 1914 to protest the Land Act?',
    ['They were ignored by the British government, which was focused on the outbreak of World War I', 'The British government immediately repealed the Land Act', 'The delegation was arrested and imprisoned', 'The British agreed to increase the reserves to 50% of the land'], 0,
    'The failure of the 1914 delegation to London showed the limitations of moderate, constitutional protest. The British government had more pressing concerns (World War I had just begun) and showed no interest in the rights of black South Africans. This pattern of being ignored by British authorities would be repeated and would eventually lead to more radical forms of resistance.'),
  t(7, '## The 1913 Land Act as the Precursor of Apartheid\n\nThe 1913 Natives\' Land Act was not just a single piece of legislation — it was the **foundation** of the entire system of racial dispossession that would reach its full development under apartheid.\n\n### How the Land Act Led to Apartheid\n\n| 1913 Land Act Principle | Apartheid Law That Extended It |\n|------------------------|-------------------------------|\n| Territorial segregation (reserves) | **Group Areas Act (1950)**: Designated areas for each racial group |\n| Restriction of African land ownership | **Bantu Authorities Act (1951)**: Formalised the homelands/Bantustans |\n| Labour tenancy and control | **Pass Laws**: Extended control over black movement and labour |\n| Separate development | **Bantustan system**: "Separate development" for each ethnic group |\n| Denial of land rights | **Prevention of Illegal Squatting Act (1951)**: Criminalised African urbanisation |\n\n### The Land Question in Democratic South Africa\n\nThe land question remains one of the most important unresolved issues in South Africa:\n\n- The **1996 Constitution** includes a **Bill of Rights** that protects property rights but also allows for land reform\n- The government has pursued **land restitution** (returning land to people dispossessed after 1913) and **land redistribution** (transferring land to previously disadvantaged groups)\n- Progress has been **slow**: by 2020, only a fraction of the land targeted for redistribution had been transferred\n- The debate about **expropriation without compensation** reflects ongoing frustration with the pace of land reform\n- The legacy of the 1913 Land Act — and the land dispossession that preceded it — continues to shape South Africa\'s economy, society, and politics\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe 1913 Natives\' Land Act stands as a stark reminder that **law can be used as an instrument of injustice**. Understanding this law and its consequences is essential for understanding South Africa\'s past — and its present.'),
  fb(8, 'The 1913 Land Act was the ___ of the apartheid system that followed decades later. The land question remains one of the most important unresolved issues in ___ South Africa.',
    ['precursor', 'democratic'],
    'Understanding the 1913 Land Act as the precursor of apartheid is crucial. The principles established in 1913 — territorial segregation, restriction of African land ownership, labour control — were systematically extended and deepened by apartheid legislation from 1948 onwards. The land question remains unresolved in democratic South Africa.'),
  t(9, '## Exam Preparation: Key Terms and Source Skills\n\n### Key Terms for the Land Act\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| **Sharecropping** | A farming arrangement where an African farmer worked white-owned land and shared the crop with the owner |\n| **Labour tenancy** | A system where Africans could live on white farms only if they provided their labour |\n| **Reserves** | Areas designated for African occupation (7.3% of land under the 1913 Act) |\n| **Migrant labour** | System where workers (usually men) left their families in rural areas to work in cities and mines |\n| **Dispossession** | The process of being deprived of land, property, and rights |\n| **Pariah** | An outcast; someone excluded from society |\n\n### Source-Based Skills Review\n\nWhen analysing sources about the Land Act:\n\n1. **Extract**: Quote directly from the source to answer "according to the source" questions\n2. **Explain**: Use your own knowledge AND the source to explain meanings and significance\n3. **Compare**: Find similarities and differences between sources (e.g., Plaatje vs government justifications)\n4. **Evaluate**: Assess reliability and usefulness — consider who wrote it, when, why, and for whom\n5. **Contextualise**: Place sources in their historical context — what was happening in South Africa in 1913?\n\n### Essay Writing: The PEEL Method\n\nRemember:\n- **P**oint: State your argument clearly\n- **E**vidence: Support with specific facts, dates, and examples\n- **E**xplanation: Explain HOW the evidence supports your point\n- **L**ink: Connect back to the essay question'),
  q(10, 'What was sharecropping and why did the 1913 Land Act target it?',
    ['Sharecropping was a farming arrangement where Africans shared crops with white landowners; the Act banned it to force Africans into labour tenancy', 'Sharecropping was a European farming technique brought to South Africa', 'Sharecropping was a system of equal land distribution', 'Sharecropping was a mining technique used on the Witwatersrand'], 0,
    'Sharecropping allowed some African farmers to maintain a degree of independence — they farmed white-owned land and shared the crop. The 1913 Act banned sharecropping because white farmers wanted Africans as cheap labourers, not independent producers. This forced formerly independent African farmers into labour tenancy, which was essentially a form of servitude.'),
];

